
	       	   backupd - a backup daemon for linux

       (C) Copyright 1998 Ullrich von Bassewitz (uz@musoftware.de)



This is a more in-depth discussion of backupd. For an overview about the
concepts and features, have a look at OVERVIEW.TXT.



Command line parameters for the server
--------------------------------------

backupd accepts one command line parameter:

    -c name    Use other config file than /etc/backupd.conf
    -V         Print version information



Command line parameters for the clients
---------------------------------------

backupc accepts some command line parameters:

    -b n       Use n KB as block size
    -h host    Connect to the named host (default is localhost)
    -l         List available resources on the server
    -p port    Connect to port instead of 12153
    -r res     Read the given resource
    -V         Print version information
    -w res     Write to the given resource

Beware: Order is important here. The command line

    backupc -h host1 -r tape1 -h host2 -r tape2

connects to host1 and requests the data from tape1, then connects to
host2 and requests the data from tape2.



Entries in /etc/backupd.conf
----------------------------

There are two types of entries in the config file (usually
/etc/backupd.conf): Global ones and resource specific ones. Global
entries will set some program options, whereas resource options say how
to handle a resource.


Global options:

Global options are all options before the first resource name (a name in
square brackets). Supported options are:

LogFacility = n

    Use n as log facility when writing to syslog. The default is
    LOG_DAEMON.

ListAllow   = "host list"
ListDeny    = "host list"

    Allow or disallow the use of the LIST command (-l switch in backupc).
    Host list is a whitespace separated list of host names (wildcards
    allowed) or ip numbers (dotted quad with an optional bit value).
    Example:

	ListAllow   = "*.mydomain.de 127.0.0.1/32"

    If an ListAllow directive exists, only hosts listed may use the LIST
    command. If the ListAllow directive does not exist, but there's a
    ListDeny directive, all hosts may use the LIST command with the
    expception of those, listed in the ListDeny list.

    If there's neither an ListAllow, nor a ListDeny directive, every host
    that is able to connect may also use the LIST command.

NoDNS = n

    Don't try to resolve the clients name. This may be an option if you
    have a dialup connection and don't want the dialout to be triggered
    by a DNS lookup. 'n' is an integer, if it is zero (the default), DNS
    lookups are done. If 'n' is not zero, backupd will not do DNS lookups
    for the client name.

    Note: If you're using access lists, you may not use names if you don't
    allow DNS lookups.


Resource options:

Each entry starts with the name of the resource in square brackets. The
matching is NOT case sensitive. Spaces in the resource name will
currently work, but this is an unwanted feature and will get removed in
an upcoming release, so don't use it.

Following the resource name, you can use one or more of the following
directives (a '*' means that the directive is mandatory). Note: String
variables are enclosed in double quotes, numbers are not.


user     = "username"
group    = "groupname"

    backupd will run as root, but will change it's uid/gid according to
    this settings, before doing actual reads and writes. Note: The
    lockfile is created *after* becoming another user, so the directory
    for the lockfile has to be writeable by this user.

    You are strongly encouraged to use these directives.


write    = "write command"             *
read     = "read command"              *

    These are the commands that backupd uses to read or write the
    resource. Only your imagination is the limit for what to put into
    these directives. backupd uses popen() to create a pipe to the
    commands, and popen uses the shell ("/bin/sh -c") to execute the
    commands, so you may put wildcards, output redirections and other
    stuff in here.

    The commands given may contain some meta characters:

      \h  is replaced by the hostname of the client
      \H  is replaced by the hostname of the client, but all characters
          that aren't allowed in filenames are changed into dashes.
      \\ is replaced by the backslash itself.

    When starting the external command, all standard file descriptors
    that aren't used are connected to /dev/null. So if you want to
    evaluate error messages, the command sends to stderr, you have to do
    that yourself. If you don't setup a special handling, the messages
    are discarded (feedback about the success of the command is given by
    the return code of the command).

    Minor gotcha:

      * There is no command line continuation. If the line gets too long
	(internal limit is 1024 chars), think about using a shell script.


lockfile = "name of lockfile"

    backupd will do resource locking if you give it the name of a
    lockfile. The name may contain the same meta characters as the read
    and write commands.

    Please note:

      * The lockfile is created after changing user/group, so the
	directory given here must be searchable/writeable by the user in
	the "user=" directive (if any).

      * Locking is done by "open (lockfile, O_CREAT | O_EXCL)". This will
	*not* work (and give all sorts of strange errors) if the lockfile
	is on a remote disk. You should use a directory on a local disk
	here.


umask = 0022

    You may give an umask value that is used when creating files (even by
    subprocesses, if not overridden). Use a leading '0' when specifying
    an octal value, use 0x for hexadecimal values.
    If you don't specify an umask, the default value (0077) applies.


allow = "host list"
deny  = "host list"

    Set up client based resource access. Host list is a whitespace
    separated list of host names (wildcards allowed) or ip numbers
    (dotted quad with an optional bit value). Example:

	allow = "*.mydomain.de 127.0.0.1/32"

    If an allow directive exists, client access is allowed if the host is
    in the list, and denied if the host is not in the list. If a there is
    no allow directive, but a deny directive, access is allowed if the
    client is not in the list.

    If there's neither an allow, nor a deny directive, access will be
    granted to every host.



Firewalling a backupd connection
--------------------------------

backupd uses one straight TCP connection to do it's work. The default
port is 12153.



Windows & OS/2 Utilities
------------------------

Thanks to Andreas Kaiser (who did the port of gtar to OS/2), there's now
a package that contains both executables (backupc and tar) that are needed
to do full backups for OS/2. The OS/2 port of gtar is very well done and
you're able to archive complete disks including extended attributes.

Unfortunately, this cannot be said about the NT port of gtar. It is not
able to read some of the system files. As usual, Microsoft itself has a
utility that is able to read *all* files (they have all the secret
information how to do that :-(), but it's GUI based and does not work
with backupc. If you know about a better gtar port, please let me know.
I've already tried the GNUWin32 port, but it crashes without any visible
cause.

The Windows gtar will also try to set creation/modification times when
doing a -xp. All these calls will fail and will give error messages. If
restoring the original times for directiories is not important for you,
you may ignore these messages (I admit it's annoying - if you have a
better tar port, please let me know).

I've already contacted the author but did not receive an answer. Since
gtar it is GNU software, I will provide also a complete binary package
for NT. I hope to have a better solution for the next release of
backupd.



Sample configurations
---------------------

An entry for a SCSI tape:

[tape0]
write  	 = "dd of=/dev/st0 bs=32k"
read 	 = "dd if=/dev/st0 bs=32k"
lockfile = "/tmp/backupd-tape0.lock"
allow    = "*.mydomain.com localhost loopback"


Say you want to backup a pool of windoze clients without much work. You
may use this entry on the server:

[daily]
user     = "backup"
group    = "backup"
write  	 = "gzip -c9 > /backup/\H"
read   	 = "gzip -cd9 < /backup/\H"
lockfile = "/tmp/backupd-\H.lock"
umask    = 0037

This will create and read compressed files with the names of the hosts
that use the resource. The lockfile used is also dependent on the name of
the client, so it is possible for more than one client to connect to this
resource at a time (you may think about compressing the data on the
clients in this case since several running gzips will give a heavily
loaded server:-). The resulting files will be owned by user/group backup
and have mode 640 (aren't readable for others).

You may want to combine this with a daily running cron script that sends
mail to the backup operator if one of the clients hasn't done a backup
for more than three days.


backupd is able to proxy itself. This may come in handy, if your clients
hide behind a firewall, or if you temporarily want to reroute a resource
(maybe your tape is broken or got moved). Use an entry like

[tape0]
write  	 = "backupc -h otherhost -w tape0"
read   	 = "backupc -h otherhost -r tape0"

to reroute the connection to the host "otherhost". Note: The \h and \H
meta characters on the final host will not expand to the real client in
this case, but to the host rerouting the resource.


You are not limited to anything like backups. Why not have a (read-only)
resource named "fortune" that is defined like this:

[fortune]
user     = "nobody"
group    = "nobody"
read   	 = "/usr/games/fortune"

Having a fortune resource is fun (and it is useful for testing the
connection to the server).



Known bugs/problems
-------------------

This software is currently BETA, so there may be lots of bugs. Here is a
list of the problems currently known:

  - The client may not always detect an error with the external command
    on the server immediately. So, if you forget to insert your tape, the
    client will tell you about it *after* your 21GB hard disk was
    transfered over your 33.6k dialup connection.

  - Data and command channel are not separate. The data channel uses a
    block counter mechanism with about 0.4% (1/256) overhead.

  - Reading and writing is currently somewhat unefficient, it causes more
    load on the server than necessary. There's some more investigation
    needed but most of the load seems to be caused by the kernel drivers.
    When doing a remote backup on a SCSI tape I get a load of about 1.20,
    but backupc itself uses only 20% of the CPU time (486/100). This is
    only true for the server. The client (backupc) does not cause
    noticeable loads when doing tape backups. Worst thing is the disk
    access.

  - backupc does work on Windows 95 as far my experience goes. The only
    problem is the MS-DOS prompt: When using pipes, it has the stupid old
    DOS behavior, that is, it writes all data into a temporary file,
    before starting the second program with the temporary file connected
    to stdin or stdout. You may want to use the tcsh port from

        ftp://ftp.blarg.net/users/amol/tcsh/

    to work around this as described in OVERVIEW.TXT.

